            {
                "entry_id": "63688fb6-2232-322c-835e-54127d13b821",
                "word": "yips",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "6fd5d98e-aba9-32b3-8512-ab3e317f5073",
                        "entry_id": "63688fb6-2232-322c-835e-54127d13b821",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A nervous condition which prevents a sportsperson from playing properly; especially a condition which causes a golfer to miss an easy putt, or a tennis player to serve a double fault."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "informal",
                            "uncountable"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "f05707fb-4a45-4cea-9a28-e5732e814e18",
                                "sense_id": "6fd5d98e-aba9-32b3-8512-ab3e317f5073",
                                "text": "His office is a baseball park and he is a pitcher for the N.Y. Mets—a job guaranteed to give anyone a good case of after-hours yips."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "52348ae5-b452-4aae-a9f8-a557e8aa0f95",
                                "sense_id": "6fd5d98e-aba9-32b3-8512-ab3e317f5073",
                                "text": "Bracing as all this may be to the President, it gives his security and logistical forces the yips."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "381bc274-55ce-48e4-baac-cb56cc893a03",
                                "sense_id": "6fd5d98e-aba9-32b3-8512-ab3e317f5073",
                                "text": "Golfers may be surprised to learn that the yips, in one form or another, occur in a wide variety of other sports and even in other non-athletic walks of life and work."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "9607f4f1-895e-4646-b18e-7cf05db83190",
                                "sense_id": "6fd5d98e-aba9-32b3-8512-ab3e317f5073",
                                "text": "Defining precisely why a professional cyclist might lose his touch on descents is as difficult as explaining a golfer's yips or a striker's sudden inability to find the net. It happens rarely, most famously in the early 1990s; the double world champion Gianni Bugno suffered from it and only rediscovered his \"flow\" after being made to listen to Mozart to calm his nerves."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
